  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis reveals a strong metabolic signal centred on **galactose metabolism and galactosemia** (FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment ~1,200×). Six of the nine input metabolites — D-glucose (C00221), myo-inositol (C00137), sucrose (C00089), galactitol (C01697), glycerol (C00116), and galactinol (C01235) — are annotated members of the KEGG *Galactose Metabolism* pathway (map00052) and its associated disease module *Galactosemia* (SMP00182). This convergence is the dominant biological signal in the dataset.\n\n**Key drivers within the galactose axis** include sucrose and galactinol, both early upstream intermediates of the Leloir pathway. Galactinol (C01235) is the activated galactose donor for glycosyltransferase reactions, and its companion galactitol (C01697) is the downstream sugar alcohol produced by aldose reductase when galactose flux is high — a classic diagnostic marker in classic galactosemia. The co-elevation of myo-inositol (C00137), which is itself a structural isomer at the intersection of galactose and inositol phosphate metabolism, suggests broad re-routing through alternative carbon sinks. KEGG graph traversal confirms that sucrose (C00089) is upstream of D-glucose (C00221) via a four-reaction path (sucrose → α-glucose-1-phosphate → UDP-glucose → UDP-galactose → D-glucose), consistent with蔗糖 cleavage feeding glycolytic entry points.\n\nA secondary, more discrete signal arises from **methionine sulfoxide (C02989)**, which anchors the KEGG *Cysteine and Methionine Metabolism* pathway (map00270). The presence of its oxidation product — rather than free methionine — is biologically meaningful: it reflects oxidative stress or metal-catalysed oxidation of the sulfur amino acid, often observed in contexts of inflammation, drug exposure, or hepatic injury. Aspirin (C01405), the only pharmaceutical in the panel, is a known cyclooxygenase inhibitor and generator of reactive oxygen species; its concurrent dysregulation may synergistically drive methionine sulfoxide accumulation via cysteine/methionine cycle perturbations.\n\nIn summary, the treatment group exhibits a coordinated perturbation of **galactose/Leloir pathway flux** with secondary **sulfur amino-acid oxidation**, consistent with a metabolic phenotype involving altered carbohydrate metabolism, oxidative stress, and — given aspirin's presence — possible pharmacological modulation of inflammatory or prostaglandin pathways. The six confirmed galactose-metabolite drivers (FDR < 0.05) make this the most statistically robust and biologically interpretable finding.",
